{"ref": "artifact://workflow/wf_58cfaf2a/root", "missing": false, "offset": 0, "limit": 4000, "total_chars": 1552, "truncated": false, "content": "**Brief (verbatim):**\n\n> Design brief: Implement loop validation as a deterministic single-pass check that detects a failing round and performs exactly one automatic redo, with no further retries.\n\n**Elaboration of the first aspect — \"loop validation as a deterministic single-pass check that detects a failing round\":**\n\nLoop validation is a single, self-contained evaluation pass that inspects the outcome of a round and returns a binary verdict — pass or fail — using only the round's observable result, with no reliance on randomness, timing, or accumulated state, so identical inputs always yield the identical verdict. Its defining duty is to reliably *detect a failing round*, meaning it must recognize the failure condition precisely and unambiguously, since it is this detection signal alone that will gate whether the one permitted automatic redo is triggered.\nDesign brief (verbatim):\n\n> Implement loop validation as a deterministic single-pass check that detects a failing round and performs exactly one automatic redo, with no further retries.\n\nSecond aspect — the \"exactly one automatic redo, with no further retries\" behavior: once the detection logic flags a round as failing, the loop triggers a single corrective re-run of that round and then stops, so the redo budget is a fixed constant (one) rather than a retry-until-success loop. This guarantees the overall run is bounded and deterministic — at most one detection-driven redo per round, and any failure surviving that redo is surfaced as terminal instead of being retried again."}

